    "identifier": "eco4cast-stac/neon4cast-stac/daily-forecasts/Beetles/Weekly_beetle_community_richness/XGBoost_detrendLM_ensemble_richness_P1W_forecast",
    "name": "XGBoost_detrendLM_ensemble",
    "description": "All forecasts for the Weekly_beetle_community_richness variable for the XGBoost_detrendLM_ensemble model. Information for the model is provided as follows: Include the names of any established algorithms or functions in a package (e.g., XGBoost, Random Forest, ARIMA, etc.) that you used.\nMy modeling approach for predicting insect 'abundance' and 'richness' involved a multi-stage process, focusing on leveraging the power of gradient boosting while explicitly addressing temporal trends and quantifying prediction uncertainty.\n\nHere's a breakdown:\n\nDetrending (Linear Regression):\nThis involved training separate LinearRegression models for each unique site_id, effectively removing individual linear trends unique to each location from the historical data.\nCore Prediction Model (XGBoost Quantile Regression):\n\nXGBoost: The primary predictive algorithm used was XGBoost (xgboost.XGBRegressor). To handle multiple target variables ('abundance' and 'richness') simultaneously, it was wrapped in sklearn.multioutput.MultiOutputRegressor.\n\nHyperparameter Tuning: To ensure optimal performance of the core prediction model, I used hyperopt's fmin function with the tpe.suggest algorithm. This process helped in finding the best hyperparameters for our XGBoost model by evaluating performance (using mean absolute error across 'abundance' and 'richness') through 5-fold cross-validation.\n\nEvaluation and Visualization: The model's performance was evaluated using sklearn.metrics.r2_score and sklearn.metrics.mean_absolute_error. \n\nUncertainty Estimation: Using 10 bootstrap ensemble models\n\nPrediction and Re-trending: After making detrended predictions on new climate data (X_predict_new) using the trained quantile XGBoost models, I re-added the site-specific linear trends (predicted using the site-specific LinearRegression models for the new data's 'datetime' values) back to the detrended quantile predictions. This resulted in final re-trended predictions, including 5th, 50th, and 95th percentiles, in the original scale of 'abundance' and 'richness'..\n                                    The model predicts this variable at the following sites: BLAN, CPER, JERC, OAES, ONAQ, SCBI, SOAP, SRER, STER, BARR, BONA, CLBJ, DCFS, DEJU, DSNY, GUAN, HARV, HEAL, KONA, NOGP, ORNL, OSBS, TEAK, TOOL, UKFS, UNDE, WOOD, WREF, YELL, BART, JORN, RMNP, SERC, SJER, STEI, TALL, TREE, ABBY, DELA, GRSM, KONZ, LAJA, LENO, MLBS, MOAB, NIWO, PUUM.\n                                    Forecasts are the raw forecasts that includes all ensemble members or distribution parameters. Due to the size of the raw forecasts, we recommend accessing the forecast summaries or scores to analyze forecasts (unless you need the individual ensemble members)",
